WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Host: Okay, good morning everyone — thanks for joining. We'll try to keep this to an hour. Quick round of intros if anyone is new; otherwise we jump straight into updates.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:25.000
Eve: Hi, Eve from Product. I'm mainly here for prioritization and to flag a couple of customer asks that might shift the roadmap.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:38.000
Sam: Sam, backend. I've got a migration plan draft and a perf question later — spoiler: hope it goes well.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:52.000
Priya: Priya, frontend. Working on the new dashboard components and a CI validator; I'll show a quick preview in the middle of the meeting.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:05.000
Liam: Liam, analytics. We finished the initial pipeline; there are some gaps in legacy event mappings I'll call out.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:25.000
Host: Great — thanks. Eve, do you want to lead with product changes and why we might reorder features? Keep it to a few minutes, then we'll go to engineering.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Eve: Sure. We did five customer interviews last week; three customers requested stronger compliance controls tied to Feature C. Long story short: if we push Feature C ahead of B, we mitigate a contract risk for a pilot customer and it unlocks a potential expansion. I know that affects scheduling, but the tradeoff seems worth it.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Host: Quick clarifying question — is the work for C mostly new or does it reuse existing pieces from B?

00:04:30.500 --> 00:06:00.000
Eve: Mostly new; there is some overlap in the auth flow with B, but the compliance controls require schema changes and an approval workflow. It's not trivial. Sam, from your side, what are the migration impacts?

00:06:00.500 --> 00:09:00.000
Sam: The big thing is a schema migration for the events model. We can do a backwards-compatible rollout with feature flags, but we will need a short maintenance window for the DB change if we do a bulk rewrite. I'd prefer a staged canary approach to avoid surprises.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:30.000
Priya: Can I jump in — if the schema changes, we need a contract with the frontend so we don't ship incompatible updates. Even minor renames break the dashboard.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:10:15.000
Sam: Absolutely. That’s why I'm proposing a versioned schema and a migration plan. We'll publish a compatibility guide and a deprecation window.

00:10:15.500 --> 00:11:00.000
Eve: Okay, action item — product will draft a proposal with business impact and a recommended priority. Engineering can respond with a migration estimate. Agreed?

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:10.000
All: Agreed.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:13:00.000
Host: Sam, can you give a quick overview of the migration timeline you'd expect for an early rollout? Rough ballpark is fine.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:15:00.000
Sam: For a safe canary-first rollout: two weeks for scripts and canary infra, one week of canary observation, and then a scheduled production cutover over a weekend if things look green. So three to four weeks end-to-end for a careful approach.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:20.000
Priya: That sounds reasonable. Frontend can align to the canary schedule — I just need the schema PRs early so the UI validators can run in CI.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:16:30.000
Liam: Quick note on data — legacy events don't have the new auditing fields. If we migrate, we'll need reconciliation jobs to backfill values or we risk reporting gaps. That could add another week for backfills depending on how we batch.

00:16:30.500 --> 00:17:00.000
Sam: Good call. We can add a backfill plan to the migration doc. I'll estimate duration based on current event volumes.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:18:00.000
Host: All right, let's shift to the dashboard preview. Priya, you mentioned a short demo — can you share the main changes and any open questions?

00:18:00.500 --> 00:20:30.000
Priya: Yes. So the major changes: component library v2 adds theming tokens and a table rewrite that supports virtualized rows. The tricky part is stabilizing the table when live updates stream in — we saw a flash/jump when new rows arrive.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:21:00.000
Priya: We tried placeholders, and that helped, but there's still a small layout shift. I'm proposing we buffer inserts for 200–300ms and batch updates to avoid jank. Thoughts?

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:40.000
Sam: Buffering sounds fine, but be cautious about observable latency. For critical alerts we shouldn't delay. Can we bypass buffering for high-priority items?

00:21:40.500 --> 00:22:10.000
Priya: Yes — we can tag events as high-priority and flush them immediately. The buffer is only for regular rows. Implementation is straightforward.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:23:00.000
Liam: From analytics side, buffering is invisible to our pipelines since we ingest raw events. As long as the UI doesn't drop events, fine. We should keep an audit log for the UI buffering so it's easier to debug user-reported issues.

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:30.000
Priya: Good idea — I'll add a debug flag and a small telemetry ping when buffering happens.

00:23:30.500 --> 00:24:00.000
Host: Okay — next, reliability and monitoring. Liam, anything from the pipeline you want to highlight?

00:24:00.500 --> 00:25:30.000
Liam: Two things: first, our daily job found a gap in legacy events mapping — about 2% of old events have null user IDs, which skews DAU. Second, dashboards now include a reconciliation panel but we need to surface it to the team more prominently.

00:25:30.500 --> 00:26:10.000
Sam: Do we know why user IDs are null? Is it upstream or a parsing issue?

00:26:10.500 --> 00:27:00.000
Liam: Early analysis says it's upstream; a version of the producer dropped the field for a short window. Backfills should fix it, but we also need an alert when schemas change upstream.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:45.000
Priya: That alert would be helpful. We could add a lightweight schema diff check in CI for producers merging changes. It would catch accidental removals.

00:27:45.500 --> 00:28:30.000
Host: Action — Priya to prototype a schema-diff validator in CI. Liam to scope the backfill and Sam to estimate the DB impact. Sound good?

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:50.000
All: Yes.

00:28:50.500 --> 00:30:00.000
Host: Quick check-in — anyone blocked right now? If not, we'll move to the tradeoff discussion on streaming infrastructure.

00:30:00.500 --> 00:32:00.000
Sam: I'll kick off. Two options on the table: self-hosted Kafka or managed streaming. Kafka gives us low-latency and consumer control; managed reduces ops but costs more and can limit fine-grained tuning. My leaning is hybrid: Kafka for the hot, low-latency paths and managed streaming for bulk ingestion.

00:32:00.500 --> 00:33:00.000
Eve: From product, speed to market matters. If managed streaming gets an important customer live faster, that's a win. Can we prototype on managed and later carve out hot-paths to Kafka if needed?

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:40.000
Sam: Yes, but prototypes must include extraction plans. We can't hard-bind to vendor-specific features without an exit strategy. That increases initial engineering effort.

00:33:40.500 --> 00:34:15.000
Priya: For the frontend, either option is transparent as long as the schemas are stable. My concern is more about delivery cadence — frequent small schema changes are disruptive.

00:34:15.500 --> 00:35:00.000
Liam: Cost-wise, managed streaming will show in monthly bills. For analytics, both options are fine. We should run a cost model for expected throughput and growth.

00:35:00.500 --> 00:35:30.000
Host: Action item — Sam to prototype hybrid approach and write a short pros/cons doc, including an estimated ops cost and time-to-implement. Eve to prioritize based on customer needs.

00:35:30.500 --> 00:36:00.000
Sam: I'll include runbook sketches for failover scenarios as well.

00:36:00.500 --> 00:37:00.000
Host: Great. Quick pause — any quick wins or blockers that can be resolved this week? Speak up now.

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:30.000
Priya: We need one small design decision on table row density; it's blocking a CSS bug. If we can decide mid-week, I can finish the mobile fixes.

00:37:30.500 --> 00:38:00.000
Eve: Product can decide. Let's default to denser rows for power users and a compact toggle for others.

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:30.000
Priya: Perfect — that'll unblock me.

00:38:30.500 --> 00:39:30.000
Liam: Also, I'll share the reconciliation dashboard link after the meeting and tag the folks who need access. Expect a short doc describing the reconciliation steps.

00:39:30.500 --> 00:40:00.000
Host: Thanks — sounds like a plan. We're about 20 minutes in. Do we want to do a 5-minute breakout to let engineering sketch PRs and then come back for decisions?

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:12.000
Sam: Yes, quick breakout sounds useful.

00:40:12.500 --> 00:45:00.000
[05-minute breakout — quiet / side conversations]

00:45:00.500 --> 00:45:30.000
Host: Back — quick status from breakout leads. Sam?

00:45:30.500 --> 00:46:20.000
Sam: We sketched the migration steps and identified the parts that need coordination: schema PRs, feature flags, and a canary environment. I have a draft timeline and will post it.

00:46:20.500 --> 00:47:00.000
Priya: I created a prototype for the CI schema diff and a small demo for buffering UI updates. I'll push the branches after this meeting.

00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:40.000
Liam: I outlined the backfill strategy and estimated compute costs for a one-time backfill; it's non-trivial but doable within a week if we parallelize jobs.

00:47:40.500 --> 00:48:20.000
Host: Excellent. Let's lock down owners and due dates. Sam — migration plan doc by next Wednesday. Priya — CI prototype and UI changes by Tuesday. Liam — backfill ETA and cost by Friday. Eve — finalize product priority and inform sales.

00:48:20.500 --> 00:49:00.000
Eve: I'll coordinate with sales and draft the priority memo.

00:49:00.500 --> 00:50:00.000
Sam: One more thing on the DB change — we should schedule a short DBA review to sign off on the maintenance window. I'll set that up.

00:50:00.500 --> 00:51:00.000
Priya: Also, small accessibility note — the new table header contrast needs a minor tweak. I'll open an issue and fix it today.

00:51:00.500 --> 00:52:00.000
Liam: I'll add a reconciliation health check to our daily jobs so we catch regressions early. Also, I'll add owners to the dashboard widgets.

00:52:00.500 --> 00:53:00.000
Host: Any risks we haven't covered? Call them out now so we can add mitigation steps.

00:53:00.500 --> 00:54:00.000
Sam: Risk: unexpected data volume growth during migration. Mitigation: throttle writes or do the migration in smaller batches and monitor.

00:54:00.500 --> 00:55:00.000
Eve: Risk: customer timeline pressure causing us to cut corners. Mitigation: product will only accelerate if engineering signs off on tradeoffs.

00:55:00.500 --> 00:56:00.000
Priya: Risk: front-end regressions due to schema drift. Mitigation: the CI schema diff and feature flags will help.

00:56:00.500 --> 00:57:00.000
Host: Good. Let's recap action items and owners so everyone's clear.

00:57:00.500 --> 00:58:20.000
Host: Recap — Sam: migration doc + DBA meeting; Priya: CI schema diff + UI fixes; Liam: backfill plan + reconciliation checks; Eve: prioritize and communicate; Product & Eng sync next Monday morning. I'll send meeting notes with owners.

00:58:20.500 --> 00:59:00.000
All: Thanks. Sounds good.

00:59:00.500 --> 01:00:00.000
Host: Perfect — thanks everyone for staying on time. If no objections, meeting adjourned. Have a great day.
